before:
Run this complete trigger example in a fresh database, separate from the notes project.
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,label TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE demo_log(old_label TEXT,new_label TEXT);
INSERT INTO demo VALUES(1,'Draft');
CREATE TRIGGER log_label AFTER UPDATE OF label ON demo
WHEN NEW.label <> OLD.label
BEGIN
 INSERT INTO demo_log(old_label,new_label) VALUES(OLD.label,NEW.label);
END;
UPDATE demo SET label='Ready'
WHERE id=1;
UPDATE demo SET label='Ready'
WHERE id=1;
SELECT old_label,new_label
FROM demo_log;
```
Only Draft/Ready is logged. The second assignment triggers the event but fails the WHEN condition.

after:
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, label TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE demo_log(old_label TEXT, new_label TEXT);
INSERT INTO demo VALUES (1, 'Draft');
CREATE TRIGGER log_label AFTER UPDATE OF label ON demo
WHEN NEW.label <> OLD.label
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO demo_log(old_label, new_label)
  VALUES (OLD.label, NEW.label);
END;
UPDATE demo SET label = 'Ready' WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE demo SET label = 'Ready' WHERE id = 1;
SELECT old_label, new_label FROM demo_log;
```
The log has one row: `Draft, Ready`. Assigning `Ready` again does not add another row.
